      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "(C)2002 American Health Foundation and Elsevier Science (USA).",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Adequate vitamin C (AsA) intake may lower the risk of arteriosclerotic cardiovascular disease, but little is known about its influence on the progression of atherogenic disease in the elderly."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We examined whether AsA intake was associated with serum lipids, apolipoprotein A-1 (ApoA1) and apolipoprotein B (ApoB), in 680 Japanese elderly persons."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There were no significant gender differences among mean serum lipids and apolipoprotein concentrations and intakes of macronutrients. AsA intake had a significant positive association with serum concentrations of high-density cholesterol and ApoA1, but an inverse association with serum concentrations of low-density cholesterol and ApoB, after adjusting for age, body mass index, total energy, and macronutrients. AsA intake was strongly inversely related to ApoA1/ApoB."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Increased AsA intake could play an important role in lipid composition and could be of potential importance in the genesis and prevention of atherogenic disease in the elderly."
          }
        ]
      },
